"# GCC removes a bounds check in the right operand of &amp;&amp;, but not in the left operand, why?\n\nThe GCC compiler removes a bounds check in the right operand of `&amp;&amp;` but not in the left operand because of the way the C and C++ language spec defines these operators. Specifically, in the C and C++ language spec, the left operand of `&amp;&amp;` is not checked for bounds, but the right operand is. This is because the left operand is a pointer, and the right operand is an integer, which is not checked for bounds.\n\nWhen the GCC compiler sees the while loop condition `g_ptrArray[ idx ] != nullptr`, it can eliminate this check because the right operand `idx` is a pointer, and the compiler knows that it will always be within the bounds of the array.\n\nOn the other hand, when the GCC compiler sees the while loop condition `g_ptrArray[ idx ] != nullptr &amp;&amp; idx &lt; ARRAY_LENGTH`, it cannot eliminate the check because the left operand `idx` is not a pointer, but rather an integer. The compiler needs to check the bounds of `idx` to ensure that it does not exceed the size of the array.\n\nThis behavior is known as a \"pointer vs. integer check\" and is a result of the way the C and C++ language spec defines these operators. It's a common source of bugs and performance issues in C and C++ code.\n\n# Explanation of the code\n\nThe code snippet you provided is a simple C/C++ program that calculates the sum of all elements in an array using a while loop. The array is defined with a fixed size of 666 elements, and the program uses an external pointer `g_ptrArray` to access the elements of the array.\n\nThe program uses two while loops: one with the left operand `g_ptrArray[ idx ]` and one with the right operand `idx`. The left operand is checked for bounds using the `&amp;&amp;` operator, while the right operand is not checked.\n\nWhen the compiler sees the first while loop, it can eliminate the check for bounds because the left operand `g_ptrArray[ idx ]` is a pointer, and the compiler knows that it will always be within the bounds of the array.\n\nWhen the compiler sees the second while loop, it cannot eliminate the check for bounds because the left operand `idx` is not a pointer, but rather an integer. The compiler needs to check the bounds of `idx` to ensure that it does not exceed the size of the array.\n\nThe compiler uses a technique called \"type inference\" to determine the type of the left and right operands of the `&amp;&amp;` and `!= nullptr` operators. By analyzing the types of the operands, the compiler can determine whether to eliminate the bounds check based on whether the left operand is a pointer or an integer.\n\n# Compiler Explorer output\n\nThe Compiler Explorer output shows the assembly code generated by the GCC compiler for the two while loops. The output shows that the first while loop has a bounds check enabled, while the second while loop does not.\n\nThe output also shows that the Compiler Explorer can eliminate the bounds check based on the type of the operands. For the first while loop, the Compiler Explorer shows that the bounds check is enabled because the left operand `g_ptrArray[ idx ]` is a pointer. For the second while loop, the Compiler Explorer shows that the bounds check is disabled because the left operand `idx` is an integer."
