"Here is the Python code that solves the problem:\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Define the schema\nassets_schema = {\n    \"id\": \"int\",\n    \"name\": \"string\",\n    \"owner\": \"string\"\n}\n\nusers_schema = {\n    \"id\": \"int\",\n    \"username\": \"string\",\n    \"ref1\": \"string\",\n    \"ref2\": \"string\"\n}\n\n# Create sample data\nassets = [\n    {\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"#1\", \"owner\": \"a\"},\n    {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"#2\", \"owner\": \"b\"},\n    {\"id\": 3, \"name\": \"#3\", \"owner\": \"c\"},\n    {\"id\": 4, \"name\": \"#4\", \"owner\": \"a\"},\n    {\"id\": 5, \"name\": \"#5\", \"owner\": \"c\"},\n    {\"id\": 6, \"name\": \"#6\", \"owner\": \"d\"},\n    {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"#7\", \"owner\": \"e\"},\n    {\"id\": 8, \"name\": \"#8\", \"owner\": \"d\"},\n    {\"id\": 9, \"name\": \"#9\", \"owner\": \"a\"},\n    {\"id\": 10, \"name\": \"#10\", \"owner\": \"a\"},\n    {\"id\": 11, \"name\": \"#11\", \"owner\": \"z\"}\n]\n\nusers = [\n    {\"id\": 1, \"username\": \"bobo\", \"ref1\": \"a\", \"ref2\": \"d\"},\n    {\"id\": 2, \"username\": \"toto\", \"ref1\": \"b\", \"ref2\": \"e\"},\n    {\"id\": 3, \"username\": \"momo\", \"ref1\": \"c\", \"ref2\": \"d\"},\n    {\"id\": 4, \"username\": \"lolo\", \"ref1\": \"a\", \"ref2\": \"f\"},\n    {\"id\": 5, \"username\": \"popo\", \"ref1\": \"c\", \"ref2\": \"f\"},\n]\n\n# Merge the assets and users tables\nmerged_data = pd.merge(assets, users, on=\"owner\", how=\"outer\")\n\n# Group by owner and count the assets\nmerged_data_grouped = merged_data.groupby(\"owner\")[\"id\"].count().reset_index()\n\n# Merge the grouped data with the ref1 and ref2 columns\nmerged_data_grouped = pd.merge(merged_data_grouped, merged_data.groupby(\"owner\")[\"ref1\"].collect(), on=\"owner\") \\\n                    .merge(merged_data_grouped, merged_data.groupby(\"owner\")[\"ref2\"].collect(), on=\"owner\")\n\n# Remove duplicates and sort by id\nmerged_data_grouped = merged_data_grouped.drop_duplicates().sort_values(by=\"id\")\n\n# Rename columns\nmerged_data_grouped = merged_data_grouped.rename(columns={\"ref1\": \"refs1\", \"ref2\": \"refs2\", \"id\": \"asset_ids\", \"asset_count\": \"asset_count\"})\n\n# Print the result\nprint(merged_data_grouped)\n```\n\nThis code first merges the `assets` and `users` tables on the `owner` column. It then groups the merged data by `owner` and counts the number of assets for each owner. Finally, it merges the grouped data with the `ref1` and `ref2` columns, removes duplicates, sorts by `id`, and renames the columns to match the schema."
