"None\nThe Raku program is producing a Seq of Array rather than a simple Array because of how the `%f` hash is being used to store and manipulate file names. In Raku, `%f` is a hash that stores file names as keys and file objects as values. When you use `push` on the `%f` hash, it adds a new key-value pair to the hash. In this case, `push` adds a new key-value pair to the `%f` hash with the file name as the key and the file object as the value.\n\nFor example, when you do `my %f; for $*HOME.dir() -&gt; $file { ... }`, it adds a new key-value pair to the `%f` hash with the file name as the key and the file object as the value. This means that the `%f` hash now contains multiple key-value pairs, where each key is a file name and each value is a file object.\n\nSince `push` adds new key-value pairs to the hash, when you do `my $p = %f.pick;`, it picks the first key-value pair from the hash, which is the file name as the key. This is why `push` is modifying the `%f` hash and producing a Seq of Array.\n\nIn contrast, a simple Array would not have the same behavior. A simple Array would contain a single key-value pair, where the key is the file name and the value is the file object. When you do `my $p = %f;`, it would pick the first key-value pair from the `%f` hash, which would be a simple Array containing the file name and the file object."
