"Here is the corrected code:\n\n```python\nfrom pandasql import sqldf\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Create a sample DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'column1': [1, 2, 3], 'column2': [4, 5, 6]})\n\n# Define the query\nquery = \"SELECT * FROM df WHERE column1 > 1\"\n\n# Execute the query using sqldf\nnew_dataframe = sqldf(query)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(new_dataframe)\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe issue with your code is that the `sqldf` function expects a connection object as an argument, but you are passing a string query. This is why you are getting the `AttributeError`.\n\nTo fix this, you need to connect to a database before executing the query. You can use the `pandas_sqlalchemy` library to connect to a database and execute queries.\n\nHere is an example of how you can modify your code to connect to a SQLite database:\n\n```python\nfrom pandasql import sqldf\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Connect to the SQLite database\nconn = pd.connect('database.db')\n\n# Create a sample DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'column1': [1, 2, 3], 'column2': [4, 5, 6]})\n\n# Define the query\nquery = \"SELECT * FROM df WHERE column1 > 1\"\n\n# Execute the query using sqldf\nnew_dataframe = sqldf(query, conn=conn)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(new_dataframe)\n```\n\nNote: You need to replace `'database.db'` with the path to your SQLite database file."
