"None\nIn Python, a list-like datatype is any collection of elements that can be indexed, sliced, and manipulated using the square bracket notation (e.g., `my_list[0]` or `my_list[1:3]`). This includes sets, dictionaries, and other types of collections that support iteration. For example:\n\n```python\nmy_set = {1, 2, 3}\nmy_dict = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 3}\n\n# my_set and my_dict are list-like\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3]\nmy_tuple = (1, 2, 3)\n```\n\nIn the context of the Pandas Series documentation, \"list-like\" refers to the fact that the `isin` method accepts a set or list of values. This allows for efficient membership testing and indexing operations."
"None\n\nThe `ToUpper` method in .NET's `System.Globalization` namespace does not require more memory when the `destination` span has the same length as the `source` span. This is because the method allocates a new span of the same length as the source span, which can be smaller than the destination span if the source span is longer.\n\nHowever, there are some edge cases to consider:\n\n- If the source span is null, the method will throw a NullReferenceException.\n- If the culture is null, the method will throw a NullReferenceException.\n- If the source span is shorter than the destination span, the method will allocate a new span of the same length as the source span, which can be larger than the destination span.\n\nTo avoid these edge cases, you should always check for null values and handle them accordingly.\n\n```csharp\npublic static string ToUpper(this ReadOnlySpan<char> source, Span<char> destination, CultureInfo culture)\n{\n    if (source == null || destination == null)\n    {\n        throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(source), \"Source and destination spans cannot be null\");\n    }\n\n    if (source.Length > destination.Length)\n    {\n        destination = stackalloc char[source.Length];\n    }\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < source.Length; i++)\n    {\n        destination[i] = char.ToUpper(source[i]);\n    }\n\n    return new string(destination);\n}\n```"
