"import pandas as pd\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\n# Create a DataFrame\ndt = pd.DataFrame({\n    'class1': ['002134', '024345', '176234'],\n    'class2': ['002003', '024234', '002004']\n})\n\n# Define the function\ndef extract_and_compare(class1, class2, class_depth):\n    return F.when(F substring(class1, 1, class_depth) == F substring(class2, 1, class_depth), True, False)\n\n# Apply the function to the DataFrame\ndt['sameclass'] = dt.apply(extract_and_compare, axis=1, args=(class1, class2, 3))\n\n# Convert the DataFrame to a Spark DataFrame\ndt_spark = dt.to_spark()\n\n# Create a DataFrame with the desired columns\ndt_spark.createDataFrame([\n    ['002134', '024345', '176234', True, True, False],\n    ['024345', '024234', '002004', True, True, False],\n    ['176234', '002003', '002004', False, False, True]\n]).toDF('class1', 'class2', 'sameclass')\n\n# Convert the Spark DataFrame back to a Pandas DataFrame\ndt_pandas = dt_spark.toPandas()\n\nprint(dt_pandas)"
"```python\nimport sys\n\ndef sort_words_by_length():\n    # Get the command line arguments\n    words = sys.argv[1:]\n\n    # Sort the words in descending order based on their lengths\n    sorted_words = sorted(words, key=len, reverse=True)\n\n    # Print the sorted words\n    print(\"\\n\".join(sorted_words))\n\n# Call the function\nsort_words_by_length()\n```\n\nThis Python code sorts the words in the command line arguments (which should be provided as a single argument) in descending order based on their lengths. It uses the `sorted` function with a custom key function `len` to sort the words by their lengths. The `reverse=True` argument ensures that the sorting is done in descending order. The sorted words are then printed to the console."
"def slist(str_list):\n    return ', '.join(str_list)\n\ndescribe 'StringTests' as follows:\n\n  It 'literal -join' {\n    &quot;1&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, &quot;3&quot; -join &quot;,&quot; | Should -Be &quot;1,2,3&quot;\n  }\n\n  It 'literal -join' {\n    @(&quot;1&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, &quot;3&quot;) -join &quot;,&quot; | Should -Be &quot;1,2,3&quot;\n  }\n\n  It 'slist returns a list of string' {\n    slist([1, 2, 3]) | Should -Be @(&quot;1&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, &quot;3&quot;)\n  }\n\n  It 'slist -join' {\n    slist(['1', '2', '3']) -join &quot;,&quot; | Should -Be &quot;1,2,3&quot;\n  }\n\n}\nNone\n\nThe reason the array is treated differently when it comes from a function return vs when it's literally declared is because of the way strings are handled in PowerShell. When a function returns an array, each element in the array is a separate string, not a single string that includes the delimiter. When a string is declared literally, it is treated as a single string that includes the delimiter."
"None\ndef append_to_dataframe(df, new_row):\n    \"\"\"\n    Appends a new dictionary to a DataFrame object.\n\n    Args:\n        df (pd.DataFrame): The DataFrame to append to.\n        new_row (dict): The dictionary to append.\n\n    Returns:\n        None\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a copy of the original DataFrame to avoid modifying it directly\n    new_df = df.copy()\n    \n    # Append the new dictionary to the copied DataFrame\n    new_df = new_df._append(new_row, ignore_index=True)\n    \n    # Return the updated DataFrame\n    return new_df"
