"```python\nimport logging\nimport threading\nimport queue\nimport signal\nimport time\n\n# Create a logger\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n\n# Create a file handler and a queue to hold log entries\nfile_handler = logging.FileHandler('log.txt')\nqueue = queue.Queue()\n\n# Set the logging level to DEBUG\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n\n# Create a formatter and attach it to the file handler\nformatter = logging.Formatter('%(asctime)s - %(name)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s')\nfile_handler.setFormatter(formatter)\n\n# Add the file handler to the logger\nlogger.addHandler(file_handler)\n\n# Define a function to process log entries in the queue\ndef process_log_entry(entry):\n    try:\n        # Process the log entry\n        print(f\"Processing log entry: {entry}\")\n        \n        # Write the log entry to file\n        queue.put(entry)\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Log any exceptions that occur during processing\n        logger.error(f\"Error processing log entry: {e}\")\n\n# Define a function to handle signals\ndef handle_signal(signum, frame):\n    try:\n        # Wait for log entries to be processed\n        while not queue.empty():\n            # Process the log entry\n            entry = queue.get()\n            process_log_entry(entry)\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Log any exceptions that occur during signal handling\n        logger.error(f\"Error handling signal: {e}\")\n\n# Register the signal handler\nsignal.signal(signal.SIGINT, handle_signal)\n\n# Create and start a new thread to process log entries\nthread = threading.Thread(target=process_log_entry, args=())\nthread.start()\n\n# Wait for the thread to finish\nthread.join()\n```\n\nThis code uses a separate thread to process log entries, which can continue to run even if the main thread crashes. The log entries are written to a file using a queue, which can be used to ensure all entries are written to disk. The signal handler is used to wait for log entries to be processed, and the main thread can continue to run without waiting for the signal."
"None\n \nThe significant difference in time consumption between the two demos lies in the nature of the tasks being performed. In the first demo, a simple arithmetic operation is performed 100,000 times sequentially, which is a sequential task. In contrast, the second demo creates five threads to perform the same task in parallel, which is a parallel task. \n\nThe reason for the significant difference in time consumption is due to the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) in C++. The GIL prevents multiple native threads from executing Python bytecodes at once, which means that only one thread can execute Python code at a time. In the first demo, all 100,000 iterations are performed sequentially, which is much slower than the parallel execution.\n\nIn the second demo, each thread executes the same task in parallel, which is much faster than sequential execution. The GIL is released when each thread completes its task, allowing the other threads to execute Python code concurrently. This is why the second demo runs much faster than the first demo, with a time consumption of approximately 18.66 seconds, compared to 23.85 seconds in the first demo."
