"Here's how you can achieve the desired effect:\n\n```python\nimport SwiftUI\n\nstruct Item: Identifiable {\n    let id = UUID()\n}\n\nstruct ContentView: View {\n    @State private var items = [Item]()\n    @State private var draggedItem: Item?\n\n    var body: some View {\n        List {\n            ForEach(items, id: \\.id) { item in\n                ZStack {\n                    Rectangle()\n                        .frame(width: 150, height: 65)\n                    Text(item.id)\n                        .padding()\n                        .background(Color.clear)\n                }\n                .onMove { from, to in\n                    items.move(fromOffsets: from, toOffset: to)\n                }\n                .onLongPressUpdate { _ in\n                    withAnimation {\n                        draggedItem = items.first(where: { $0.id == item.id }) ?? nil\n                    }\n                }\n                .onDrop { _ in\n                    withAnimation {\n                        draggedItem = items.first(where: { $0.id == item.id }) ?? nil\n                    }\n                    items.remove(at: items.firstIndex(of: item.id))\n                }\n            }\n            .listRowSeparator(.hidden)\n            .listRowBackground(Color.clear)\n        }\n        .listStyle(.plain)\n        .scrollContentBackground(.hidden)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis code achieves the desired effect by using a `ZStack` to stack the rounded rectangles on top of each other, and then using the `onLongPressUpdate` and `onDrop` modifiers to animate the movement and dropping of the item. The `onMove` modifier is used to move the item to the new position, and the `onDrag` modifier is used to animate the movement when the item is being dragged. The `onLongPressUpdate` and `onDrop` modifiers are used to set the `draggedItem` variable when the item is being dragged or dropped."
