"```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\nnames = [['Pat','Sam', pd.NA, 'Tom', ''], ['Angela', pd.NA, 'James', '.', 'Jackie']]\nvalues = [[1, 9, 1, 2, 1], [1, 3, 1, 5, 10]]\n\ndef find_nan_indices(names, values):\n    # Convert lists to numpy arrays for easier manipulation\n    names = np.array(names)\n    values = np.array(values)\n\n    # Find indices of NaN values\n    all_nan_idx = []\n    for idx, name in enumerate(names):\n        if pd.isnull(name):\n            all_nan_idx.append(idx)\n\n    # Remove NaN values from the lists\n    new_names = names[all_nan_idx]\n    new_values = values[all_nan_idx]\n\n    return new_names, new_values\n\nnew_names, new_values = find_nan_indices(names, values)\n\nprint(\"New Names:\")\nprint(new_names)\nprint(\"New Values:\")\nprint(new_values)\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `find_nan_indices` that takes two lists (names and values) as input. It converts the lists to numpy arrays for easier manipulation. It then finds the indices of NaN values in the original lists and removes them. The function returns the new lists with NaN values removed. The code also includes an example usage of the function."
