\")\n\natob_ala = [i if (abs(lo-n), i) < (abs(hi-n), j) else j for ((lo,i),(hi,j)), n in zip(rb,a)]\nprint(f\"index a to b (Alain): {atob_ala}, type: {type(atob_ala)}\")\nprint(f\"running time (Alain): {time.time() - 2.178112030029297} seconds\")\n\natob_ken = [find_nearest(ai) for ai in a]\nprint(f\"index a to b (ken): {atob_ken}, type: {type(atob_ken)}\")\nprint(f\"running time (ken): {time.time() - 2.663684368133545} seconds\")\n```\n\nThese methods should provide a good way to find the indices of elements in a list that are present in another list. However, keep in mind that the performance may vary depending on the size of the input lists and the specific use case."
