"None\n \n Time complexity is a measure of an algorithm's performance, not its practicality. While it's true that some algorithms with high time complexity may be used in specific situations, such as scientific simulations or data processing, they are rarely used for small inputs like n < 1000 in practice.\n\nThis is because the overhead of running an algorithm with high time complexity can outweigh its benefits, especially when the input size is small. For instance, if algorithm_1 has a time complexity of O(n^2) and algorithm_2 has a time complexity of O(n), but for a large input, algorithm_2 may be faster due to its lower overhead. However, for small inputs, algorithm_1 may still be faster due to its lower overhead.\n\nMoreover, in most real-world applications, the input size is typically small, and the algorithm's performance is critical. In such cases, it's more important to focus on optimizing the algorithm's implementation rather than its time complexity."
