"You're trying to extract the RGB buffer from an I420 frame using FFmpeg. The issue is likely due to the way you're accessing the pixel data.\n\nThe `YCrCb_to_RGB8` function is extracting the Y, Cr, and Cb components from the input frame, but you're not correctly extracting the RGB components.\n\nTo fix this, you should extract the RGB components from the `YCrCb` frame, which is the first frame in the I420 frame. The `YCrCb` frame is a 3-channel frame, where the first channel is the Y component, the second channel is the Cr component, and the third channel is the Cb component.\n\nHere's the corrected code:\n\n```cpp\ninline void YCrCb_to_RGB8(int Y, int Cr, int Cb, int&amp; R, int&amp; G, int&amp; B){\n    R = Y;\n    G = Y + 1.402 * (Cr - 128);\n    B = Y - 0.344136 * (Cb - 128) - 0.71414 * (Cr - 128);\n}\n\nint getRGB8buffer(AVFrame* pFrame, byte* buffer){\n    const int width = pFrame->width, height = pFrame->height;\n    int Y, Cr, Cb;\n    int R, G, B;\n\n    int pixel = 0;\n    for (int y = 0; y < height; y++) {\n        for (int x = 0; x < width; x++) {\n            Y = pFrame->data[0][x + y * width];\n            Cr = pFrame->data[1][x / 2 + ((int) (y / 2)) * pFrame->linesize[1]];\n            Cb = pFrame->data[2][x / 2 + ((int) (y / 2)) * pFrame->linesize[2]];\n\n            YCrCb_to_RGB8(Y, Cr, Cb, R, G, B);\n            buffer[pixel * 3 + 0] = R;\n            buffer[pixel * 3 + 1] = G;\n            buffer[pixel * 3 + 2] = B;\n            pixel++;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this corrected code, we extract the Y, Cr, and Cb components from the `YCrCb` frame and use them to calculate the corresponding RGB components."
"```python\nimport React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [leftTranslateX, setLeftTranslateX] = useState(0);\n  const [rightTranslateX, setRightTranslateX] = useState(0);\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState(400);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const handleResize = () => {\n      setLeftTranslateX(width / 2 - 100);\n      setRightTranslateX(width / 2 + 100);\n    };\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\n    return () => {\n      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  const leftSplash = 'left-splash.jpg';\n  const rightSplash = 'right-splash.jpg';\n\n  return (\n    <View style={{ flexDirection: 'row', width: '100%' }}>\n      <Animated.View\n        style={{\n          width: width / 2,\n          height: '100%',\n          justifyContent: 'flex-end',\n          alignItems: 'flex-end',\n          transform: [{ translateX: leftTranslateX }],\n          backgroundColor: 'blue',\n        }}\n      >\n        <Image\n          source={leftSplash}\n          style={{ width: '100%', height: '100%', alignSelf: 'flex-end' }}\n          resizeMode=\"contain\"\n        />\n      </Animated.View>\n      <Animated.View\n        style={{\n          width: width / 2,\n          height: '100%',\n          justifyContent: 'flex-start',\n          alignItems: 'flex-start',\n          transform: [{ translateX: rightTranslateX }],\n          backgroundColor: 'red',\n        }}\n      >\n        <Image\n          source={rightSplash}\n          style={{ width: '100%', height: '100%', alignSelf: 'flex-start' }}\n          resizeMode=\"contain\"\n        />\n      </Animated.View>\n    </View>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;\n```"
"```python\nimport Oolong from '../assets/img/Oolong.png'\nimport ChaiTea from '../assets/img/ChaiTea.png'\nimport CharcoalRoastedOolong from '../assets/img/CharcoalRoastedOolong.png'\nimport HighMountainOolong from '../assets/img/HighMountainOolong.png'\nimport RubyBlackTea from '../assets/img/RubyBlackTea.png'\n\nimages = {\n    'Oolong': Oolong,\n    'Chai Tea': ChaiTea,\n    'Charcoal Roasted Oolong': CharcoalRoastedOolong,\n    'High Mountain Oolong': HighMountainOolong,\n    'Ruby Black Tea': RubyBlackTea,\n}\n\ndef export_images():\n    return images\n\ndef main():\n    print(export_images()['Oolong'])\n    print(export_images()['Chai Tea'])\n    print(export_images()['Charcoal Roasted Oolong'])\n    print(export_images()['High Mountain Oolong'])\n    print(export_images()['Ruby Black Tea'])\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThe `@fs` in the image path is likely an alias for the `fileSystem` module, which is a built-in module in Node.js. When you use `import Oolong from '../assets/img/Oolong.png'`, you are importing the image file from a local file system path. The `@fs` part of the path is likely a shortcut for the `fileSystem` module.\n\nIn your case, when you run the script, it imports the image files from the local file system path `../assets/img/Oolong.png`, which includes the `@fs` part. This is why you see the `@fs` error message.\n\nTo avoid this issue, you can simply remove the `@fs` part from your image path, like this:\n\n```python\nimport Oolong from '../assets/img/Oolong.png'\nimport ChaiTea from '../assets/img/ChaiTea.png'\nimport CharcoalRoastedOolong from '../assets/img/CharcoalRoastedOolong.png'\nimport HighMountainOolong from '../assets/img/HighMountainOolong.png'\nimport RubyBlackTea from '../assets/img/RubyBlackTea.png'\n\nimages = {\n    'Oolong': Oolong,\n    'Chai Tea': ChaiTea,\n    'Charcoal Roasted Oolong': CharcoalRoastedOolong,\n    'High Mountain Oolong': HighMountainOolong,\n    'Ruby Black Tea': RubyBlackTea,\n}\n\ndef export_images():\n    return images\n\ndef main():\n    print(export_images()['Oolong'])\n    print(export_images()['Chai Tea'])\n    print(export_images()['Charcoal Roasted Oolong'])\n    print(export_images()['High Mountain Oolong'])\n    print(export_images()['Ruby Black Tea'])\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"
"```python\nimport os\nimport glob\n\ndef get_collection_images(collection):\n    \"\"\"\n    Dynamically import all images from a folder in Astro.\n\n    Args:\n        collection (str): The name of the collection.\n\n    Returns:\n        list: A list of image file paths.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Construct the path to the collection folder\n    collection_folder = f\"public/img/{collection}\"\n\n    # Check if the collection folder exists\n    if not os.path.exists(collection_folder):\n        raise FileNotFoundError(f\"The collection folder '{collection_folder}' does not exist.\")\n\n    # Get a list of image file paths\n    image_paths = glob.glob(os.path.join(collection_folder, \"*.jpg\", \"*.png\", \"*.gif\"))\n\n    # Map each image file path to its corresponding image source\n    images = {imgSrc: f\"<img src='{imgSrc}' />\" for imgSrc in image_paths}\n\n    return images\n```"
"None\nYou are correct that the `Page` object in `fitz` does not have an `getImageList` method. The `getImageList` method is actually a part of the `Image` object, not the `Page` object. \n\nTo extract images from a PDF, you should use the `Image` object from `fitz` directly. Here's the corrected code:\n\n```python\nimport fitz \n\ndef extract_images_from_pdf(file_path):\n    # Open the PDF file\n    with fitz.open(file_path) as pdf_file:\n        # Iterate over each page\n        for page_index in range(len(pdf_file)):\n            # Get the page itself\n            page = pdf_file[page_index]\n            # Iterate over each image on the page\n            for image_index, img in enumerate(page.get_images(), start=1):\n                # Print the image index and the image itself\n                print(f\"Image {image_index}: {img}\")\n                # Save the image to local disk\n                page.save(f\"image{page_index+1}_{image_index}.{page.get_ext()[1]}\", format=page.get_format())\n\n# Usage\nextract_images_from_pdf(\"my_file_pdf.pdf\")\n```"
