"import numpy as np\n\nprint(\"Why is np.dot so much faster than np.sum?\")\nprint(\"Following this answer we know that np.sum is slow and has faster alternatives.\")\nprint(\"For example:\")\nprint(\"  In [20]: A = np.random.rand(1000)\")\nprint(\"In [21]: B = np.random.rand(1000)\")\nprint(\"In [22]: %timeit np.sum(A)\")\nprint(\"3.21 \u00b5s \u00b1 270 ns per loop (mean \u00b1 std. dev. of 7 runs, 100,000 loops each)\")\nprint(\"In [23]: %timeit A.sum()\")\nprint(\"1.7 \u00b5s \u00b1 11.5 ns per loop (mean \u00b1 std. dev. of 7 runs, 1,000,000 loops each)\")\nprint(\"In [24]: %timeit np.add.reduce(A)\")\nprint(\"1.61 \u00b5s \u00b1 19.6 ns per loop (mean \u00b1 std. dev. of 7 runs, 1,000,000 loops each)\")\nprint(\"But all of them are slower than:\")\nprint(\"  In [25]: %timeit np.dot(A,B)\")\nprint(\"1.18 \u00b5s \u00b1 43.9 ns per loop (mean \u00b1 std. dev. of 7 runs, 1,000,000 loops each)\")\n\n# Check if np.dot is faster than np.sum\nA = np.random.rand(1000)\nB = np.random.rand(1000)\nnp_sum_time = A.sum()\ndot_time = np.dot(A, B)\n\nif dot_time < np_sum_time:\n    print(\"np.dot is faster than np.sum\")\nelse:\n    print(\"np.sum is faster than np.dot\")"
"None\n \nThe issue lies in how the `uint64` data type is being used in the context of negative values. When you try to subtract 1 from an array of `uint64` values, NumPy performs integer division instead of subtraction, which discards the fractional part and returns the integer part with the sign. This is because the `uint64` data type is an unsigned integer type, and subtracting 1 from it would essentially be equivalent to performing an unsigned subtraction, which is not what you want.\n\nIn Python 3, the `int` data type is also an unsigned integer type. When you try to subtract 1 from an `int`, it performs a signed subtraction, which discards the fractional part and returns the integer part with the sign. This is why you're seeing the expected output of `0xFFFF'FFFF'FFFF'FFFE` when you subtract 1 from an array of `uint64` values.\n\nTo fix this issue, you can use the `numpy.int64` data type, which is an unsigned integer type that supports signed and unsigned operations. Alternatively, you can use the `numpy.int32` data type, which is a signed integer type that can handle negative numbers."
