"```java\nimport java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executors;\n\npublic class MultithreadedSum {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int a = 1000000; // range of interest\n        int b = 2000000; // end of range\n        int numThreads = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(); // number of available threads\n        int[] f = new int[a + 1]; // function to sum\n\n        // initialize the function values\n        for (int i = 0; i <= a; i++) {\n            f[i] = i;\n        }\n\n        // divide the range into chunks using multithreading\n        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(numThreads);\n        int chunkSize = Math.min(a, b) / numThreads;\n        int chunkStart = a - chunkSize;\n        int chunkEnd = Math.min(chunkStart + chunkSize, b);\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {\n            int start = i * chunkSize + chunkStart;\n            int end = Math.min((i + 1) * chunkSize, chunkEnd);\n            executor.submit(() -> {\n                for (int n = start; n < end; n++) {\n                    f[n] = f[n] + f[n - chunkSize];\n                }\n            });\n        }\n\n        // sum the values in parallel\n        long sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {\n            sum += f[i];\n        }\n\n        System.out.println(sum);\n    }\n}\n```"
"```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Using continue/return statement inside .ForEach() method - is it better to use foreach ($item in $collection) instead?\ndef test continue_return():\n    # Create a large list of numbers\n    numbers = list(range(100000))\n\n    # Using foreach ($item in $collection)\n    collection = itertools.count(0)\n    result = []\n    for item in collection:\n        # Check if the speed advantage of the .ForEach() method is too big to settle for foreach\n        if len(result) < 10000:\n            # Using continue/return statement inside .ForEach() method\n            collection = itertools.islice(collection, 0, 10000)\n            result = []\n            for item in collection:\n                # Check if the speed advantage of the .ForEach() method is too big to settle for foreach\n                if len(result) < 10000:\n                    # Using continue/return statement inside .ForEach() method\n                    result = itertools.chain.from_iterable([item] * 10)\n        else:\n            # Using foreach ($item in $collection)\n            result = []\n            for item in collection:\n                # Check if the speed advantage of the .ForEach() method is too big to settle for foreach\n                if len(result) < 10000:\n                    # Using foreach ($item in $collection)\n                    result = list(itertools.islice(itertools.chain.from_iterable(numbers), 0, 10000))\n\n    return result\n\n# Testing the function\nresult = test_continue_return()\nprint(result)\n```"
"vapply() is slower than sapply() in the following circumstances:\n\n* When the data contains large objects (e.g., lists, matrices, or data frames with many rows) that are not hashable, because hashable objects are subject to garbage collection, which can lead to slower performance.\n* When the data contains objects that are not hashable, because hashable objects are subject to garbage collection, which can lead to slower performance.\n* When the data contains objects with a large number of references to themselves, because this can lead to slower performance due to increased garbage collection.\n* When the data is not sorted, because sorting can be expensive and can lead to slower performance.\n* When the data contains objects with a large number of unique elements, because this can lead to slower performance due to increased garbage collection."
"Time Complexity Analysis of Recursive Function with Recursive Call in the Loop\n====================================================================\n\nTo determine the time complexity of the given recursive function `fun`, we need to analyze the number of operations performed at each level of the recursion tree.\n\n```python\ndef fun(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Recursive function to calculate the factorial of a given integer.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The input integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        None\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0 or n == 1:\n        # Base case: Factorial of 0 and 1 is 1\n        return\n    else:\n        # Recursive call: Calculate the factorial of n/2\n        fun(n // 2)\n        # Print the current value of n\n        print(n)\n\n# Example usage:\nfun(5)\n```\n\nIn this recursive function, each call to `fun` calculates the factorial of a smaller input (`n/2`) and then recursively calls `fun` again on the smaller input. The time complexity of this function is determined by the number of levels in the recursion tree.\n\nObserving the given code, the maximum height of the recurrence tree would be `logn`, and from Level 1 to Level `logn`, there would be `n-k` calls on each level, where `k` is the number of levels in the recursion tree. However, we can simplify the recurrence equation by using the property of the factorial function: `T(n) = T(n/2) + T((n-1)/2) + ... + T(1)`.\n\nTo solve this, we can use the following approach:\n\n*   We can rewrite the recurrence equation as `T(n) = T(1) + T(2) + ... + T(n-1)`.\n*   Each recursive call adds `n-k` new calls to the previous level, where `k` is the number of levels in the recursion tree.\n*   Since each call adds `n-k` new calls, the total number of calls at each level is proportional to `n` and `k`.\n*   Therefore, the time complexity of the function is `O(n log n)`.\n\nNote that the constant factor `O(1)` in the time complexity is ignored because it does not affect the overall complexity."
