"None\n```python\nimport markdown\nimport json\n\ndef display_markdown_content(markdown_content):\n    \"\"\"Converts markdown content to HTML.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Try to convert markdown content to HTML\n        html = markdown.markdown(markdown_content)\n        return html\n    except Exception as e:\n        # If conversion fails, return the original markdown content\n        return markdown_content\n\ndef render_nuxt3(markdown_content):\n    \"\"\"Renders markdown content for Nuxt3 application.\"\"\"\n    # Display markdown content as HTML tags\n    html = display_markdown_content(markdown_content)\n    # Render HTML content in Nuxt3 application\n    return html\n\n# Example usage\nmarkdown_content = \"\"\"\n<h2 class=\"bg-slate-100 p-5 mt-5 border rounded\">Test Title</h2>\n<p class=\"bg-slate-100 p-5 mt-5 border rounded\">Test Text</p>\n\"\"\"\n\nhtml = render_nuxt3(markdown_content)\nprint(html)\n```"
"Here's a more robust and maintainable way of implementing the API call using Dio package in Flutter.\n\n```dart\nimport 'package:dio/dio.dart';\nimport 'package:http/http.dart' as http;\n\nclass CreateAccountRepository {\n  static final Dio _dio = Dio();\n\n  static final String _baseUrl = 'https://your-api-endpoint.com/api/v1/';\n\n  factory CreateAccountRepository() => CreateAccountRepository._();\n\n  Future<void> userRegistration(\n      AccountCreateRequest requestParams) async {\n    try {\n      final response = await _dio.post(\n        _baseUrl + 'users',\n        data: requestParams.toJson(),\n        headers: {\n          'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n        },\n      );\n\n      if (response.statusCode == 200) {\n        final user = User.fromJson(jsonDecode(response.body));\n        return user;\n      } else {\n        throw Exception('Failed to load user');\n      }\n    } catch (e) {\n      throw Exception('Failed to load user: $e');\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nclass AccountCreateRequest {\n  final String name;\n  final String email;\n  final String password;\n  final String phone;\n}\n\nclass User {\n  final int id;\n  final String name;\n  final String email;\n  final String password;\n  final String phone;\n  final bool isSuccess;\n\n  User({this.id, this.name, this.email, this.password, this.phone, this.isSuccess});\n}\n\nclass CreateAccountResponse {\n  final bool isSuccess;\n  final String message;\n\n  CreateAccountResponse({this.isSuccess, this.message});\n\n  factory CreateAccountResponse.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {\n    return CreateAccountResponse(\n      isSuccess: json['success'],\n      message: json['message'],\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis implementation provides a more robust and maintainable way of making API calls to the specified endpoint. The Dio package is used to handle the HTTP request, and the `CreateAccountRepository` class encapsulates the API logic. The `AccountCreateRequest` and `User` classes are used to deserialize the API response.\n\nYou can use the `CreateAccountRepository` class like this:\n\n```dart\nvoid main() async {\n  final repository = CreateAccountRepository();\n  final requestParams = AccountCreateRequest(\n    name: 'John Doe',\n    email: 'johndoe@example.com',\n    password: 'password123',\n    phone: '1234567890',\n  );\n  final user = await repository.userRegistration(requestParams);\n  print(user);\n}\n```\n\nReplace the `AppUrl.pathRegister` and `AppStrings.ERROR_INTERNET_CONNECTION` variables with the actual URL and error message you want to display."
"You can achieve this by using a global configuration in ASP.NET Core 6+. Here's an example of how you can do it:\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\nclass Configuration:\n    def __init__(self, appSettings: dict):\n        self.appSettings = appSettings\n\n    def configure(self):\n        logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\n        appSettings = self.appSettings\n        route_pattern = r\"^(\\w+)\\s*[-_a-zA-Z0-9]+$\"\n        \n        for controller in appSettings[\"routes\"]:\n            controller_name = controller[\"name\"].lower()\n            route = f\"/{controller_name.replace('-', '_')}\"\n            \n            if not route.startswith(\"/\"):\n                route = f\"/{route}\"\n            \n            self.appSettings[\"routes\"][controller_name] = route\n            \n            if controller_name.startswith(\"package-\"):\n                self.appSettings[\"routes\"][controller_name] = f\"/package-groups/{controller_name.replace('-', '_')}\"\n```\n\nIn this code, we're using a custom configuration class `Configuration` that loads the app settings from the `appsettings.json` file. We're then iterating over the routes defined in the app settings, and for each controller, we're generating the corresponding route in kebab-case format. We're also checking if the controller name starts with \"package-\" to ensure that we're generating the correct route for package groups endpoints.\n\nTo use this configuration, you'll need to add the following code to your `Program.cs` file:\n\n```python\nvar builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);\n\nvar app = builder.Build();\n\n// Load app settings from appsettings.json\nvar appSettings = new Configuration(appsettings);\nappSettings.configure();\n\n// Define routes\napp.UseRouting();\napp.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>\n{\n    endpoints.MapControllers();\n});\n\napp.Run();\n```\n\nThis will configure the routes for all controllers in your project, and you can access them by using the \"/package-groups\" endpoint."
