"None\nIn Python, a list-like object is an object that can be iterated over using a for loop or the enumerate function, and also supports the indexing and slicing operations. This includes sets, dictionaries, and any other type of object that supports these operations.\n\nFor example, the following are list-like objects:\n\n- `list`: a built-in list type\n- `tuple`: a tuple type\n- `set`: a set type\n- `dict`: a dictionary type\n- `str`: a string type\n- `bytes`: a bytes type\n- `range`: a range object\n- `enumerate`: a function that returns an iterator yielding tuples containing a count (from start which defaults to 0) and the values obtained from iterating over the sequence."
