"```python\nclass Parent:\n    def method(self):\n        print(\"Parent\")\n\nclass Child(Parent):\n    def method(self):\n        print(\"Child\")\n\nchild = Child()\nprint(child.method())  # Outputs: Child\n```\n\nWhen the code is run, it creates an instance of the `Child` class and calls the `method` method on it. The `method` method in the `Parent` class has a method parameter `self`, which is a reference to the instance of the class. This is the same as in JavaScript, where a function can take an object as an argument.\n\nIn the `Child` class, the `method` method has the same name as a method in the `Parent` class, which means Python will look for a method with that name in the `Child` class's namespace. Since the `method` method in the `Parent` class has a different name (`self.method`), Python will look for a method with that name in the `Child` class's namespace, which is why it returns the `method` method from the `Parent` class.\n\nThis is an example of a technique called \"method overloading\" or \"method overriding\", where a class can provide multiple methods with the same name, but different parameters or behaviors."
