"```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\ndef check_leap_year(year):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given year is a leap year.\n    \n    Args:\n    year (int): The year to check.\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the year is a leap year, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)\n\ndef validate_date(date_string, year):\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates a given date string against a specified year.\n    \n    Args:\n    date_string (str): The date string to validate.\n    year (int): The year to validate against.\n    \n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the date is valid, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        date = datetime.strptime(date_string, \"%d/%m/%Y\")\n        if date.year == year:\n            return True\n        elif check_leap_year(year):\n            date = datetime(date.year + 1, 2, 29)  # February 29th in leap year\n        else:\n            date = datetime(date.year + 1, 2, 28)  # February 28th in non-leap year\n        return date.day == 29\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n\n# Example usage:\ndate_string = \"29/02/2024\"\nyear = 2024\nprint(validate_date(date_string, year))\n```\n\nThis code defines two helper functions: `check_leap_year` checks if a given year is a leap year, and `validate_date` validates a given date string against a specified year. The `validate_date` function first tries to parse the date string into a `datetime` object. If the date is valid, it checks if the year matches the specified year. If not, it checks if the year is a leap year and adjusts the date accordingly. If the date is still invalid, it returns False. If the date is valid, it returns True."
"None\n\nThe given line of code `let dateIsInFuture = (optionalDate ?? .now) >= .now` is not bad form. The `??` operator in Swift is used for \"or else\" and it will return the second operand if the first operand is `nil`. In this case, it will return the second operand (the current time) if `optionalDate` is `nil`, and the first operand (the current time) otherwise. This means that the condition `(optionalDate ?? .now) >= .now` will evaluate to `true` if `optionalDate` is `nil` and `true` otherwise, which is equivalent to the logical \"or\" operator."
"```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <date/date.h>\n\nint main()\n{\n    auto currentDate = date::floor<date::days>(std::chrono::system_clock::now());\n    std::cout << \"Current Date: \" << date::format(\"%F\", currentDate) << std::endl;\n\n    date::year_month_day mondayDate = currentDate - (date::weekday(currentDate) - date::Monday);\n    std::cout << \"Monday: \" << date::format(\"%F\", mondayDate) << std::endl;\n\n    date::year_month_day sundayDate = currentDate + date::days{ date::Sunday.c_encoding() };\n    std::cout << \"Sunday: \" << date::format(\"%F\", sundayDate) << std::endl;\n\n    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < date::Sunday.c_encoding(); i++) {\n        date::year_month_day nextDate = sundayDate + date::days{ date::Sunday.c_encoding() };\n        std::cout << \"Future Date: \" << date::format(\"%F\", nextDate) << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    std::cin.get();\n    return 0;\n}\n```"
"Here is the Python code that parses the datetime strings according to the specified patterns:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone, ZonedDateTime\nfrom dateutil import parser\n\ndef parse_datetime(date_string, dtf):\n    \"\"\"Parse a datetime string according to the specified pattern.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        dt = parser.parse(date_string, dtf=dtf)\n        return dt\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n\ndef main():\n    # Define the datetime patterns\n    patterns = [\n        r\"(\\d{4})\",\n        r\"(\\d{1,2})\",\n        r\"(\\d{1,2})[T][0-9:]*\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})-(\\d{1,2})[T][0-9:]*\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})-(\\d{1,2})T(\\d{2,4})\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})-(\\d{1,2})T(\\d{2,4})[+-]\\s*(\\d{2,4})\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})-(\\d{1,2})Z\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})-(\\d{1,2})T(\\d{2,4})[+-]\\s*(\\d{2,4})\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})-(\\d{1,2})T(\\d{2,4})[+-]\\s*(\\d{2,4})[+-][0-9:]*\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})-(\\d{1,2})[+-]\\s*(\\d{2,4})[+-][0-9:]*\",\n        r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{1,2})GMT[+-]\\s*(\\d{2,4})\"\n    ]\n\n    # Define the datetime formatter\n    dtf = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(r\"['-]MM['-']dd['-']THH[':']mm[':']ss'.' + r\"\\s*Z\" + r\"\\s*[O][V][V]\")\n    dtf = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(r\"['-]MM['-']dd['-']THH[':']mm[':']ss'.' + r\"\\s*[O][V][V]\")\n    dtf = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(r\"['-]MM['-']dd['-']THH[':']mm[':']ss'.' + r\"\\s*[O][V][V]\" + r\"\\s*[+-]\\s*Z\")\n    dtf = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(r\"['-]MM['-']dd['-']THH[':']mm[':']ss'.' + r\"\\s*[O][V][V]\" + r\"\\s*[+-]\\s*[03:00|03:30|03:45|12:00|12:30|12:45|15:00|15:30|15:45|18:00|18:30|18:45|21:00|21:30|21:45]\")\n\n    # Parse the datetime strings\n    for date_string in [\n        \"2015\",\n        \"2015-01\",\n        \"2015-01-01\",\n        \"2015-02-03T00\",\n        \"2015-02-03T00:30\",\n        \"2015-02-03T00:30:50\",\n        \"2015-02-03T00:3