"```python\ndef get_lt_gt_signature(obj, key):\n    \"\"\"\n    Get the `&lt; ... &gt;` syntax on an object.\n    \n    This function is similar to the Raku example provided in the documentation.\n    It automatically converts the `&lt; ... &gt;` syntax into a Str when the key is a Str.\n    \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(key, str):\n        return f\"&lt;{key}&gt;\"\n    else:\n        return \"&lt; {} &gt; \".format(key)\n\n# Example usage\nclass A:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.x = None\n\n    def CALL-ME(self, key):\n        self.x = get_lt_gt_signature(key)\n\na = A()\na.CALL-ME(\"one two three\")\nprint(a.x)  # Output: two\na.CALL-ME(\"two\")\nprint(a.x)  # Output: two\n```"
"```python\ndef compare_pointers():\n    # Create objects of different classes\n    struct_B = B()\n    struct_B1 = B1()\n    struct_D = D()\n\n    # Create a pointer to struct_D\n    d_ptr = struct_D\n\n    # Create a pointer to struct_B\n    b_ptr = d_ptr\n\n    # Compare the pointers\n    assert(d_ptr == b_ptr)\n\n# Define classes\nclass B:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.a = 0\n\nclass B1:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.b = 0\n\nclass D : B, B1\n```\n\nThe code above demonstrates why a pointer to a base class with a different address compares equal to the derived class pointer. This is because the address of an object refers to the memory location where the object is stored, not the object itself. When a pointer is assigned a value that represents the address of an object, it points to that memory location, not the object itself. \n\nIn the given example, `d_ptr` and `b_ptr` both point to the same memory location, which is the memory location where the `struct_D` object is stored. Therefore, the code `assert(d_ptr == b_ptr)` will not fail. \n\nThis is why technically `d_ptr` and `b_ptr` point to objects with different addresses, despite both being derived classes of `struct_D`."
