"```csharp\nimport os\n\n# Define the runtime identifier for Windows 10\nWIN10RuntimeIdentifier = \"win10-x86\"\nWIN10RuntimeIdentifier64 = \"win10-x64\"\nWIN10RuntimeIdentifierArm64 = \"win10-arm64\"\n\n# Define the target framework for .NET 8\nTARGETFramework = \"net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0\"\n\n# Define the .csproj file\ncsproj_file = \"\"\"\n<PropertyGroup>\n    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>\n    <TargetFramework>{}</TargetFramework>\n    <TargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.17763.0</TargetPlatformMinVersion>\n    <RootNamespace>SudokuEngineApp</RootNamespace>\n    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>\n    <Platforms>x86;x64;ARM64</Platforms>\n    <RuntimeIdentifiers>{}</RuntimeIdentifiers>\n    <PublishProfile>win10-$(Platform).pubxml</PublishProfile>\n    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>\n    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>\n</PropertyGroup>\n\"\"\".format(TARGETFramework)\n\n# Define the output file name\noutput_file_name = \"SudokuEngineApp.exe\"\n\n# Try to build the project with the updated .csproj file\ntry:\n    # Use the subprocess module to run the build command\n    subprocess.run([\"dotnet\", \"build\", \"-o\", output_file_name], cwd=\"SudokuEngineApp\", check=True)\n\n    # Read the output of the build command\n    build_output = subprocess.check_output([\"dotnet\", \"publish\", \"-o\", output_file_name], cwd=\"SudokuEngineApp\").decode(\"utf-8\")\n\n    # Check if the build command failed\n    if \"NETSDK1083: The specified RuntimeIdentifier\" in build_output:\n        print(\"The specified RuntimeIdentifier is not recognized.\")\n    elif \"NETSDK1083: The specified RuntimeIdentifier\" not in build_output:\n        print(\"The specified RuntimeIdentifier is not recognized.\")\n\n    # Check if the RuntimeIdentifiers are recognized\n    if WIN10RuntimeIdentifier not in build_output:\n        print(\"WIN10RuntimeIdentifier is not recognized.\")\n    if WIN10RuntimeIdentifier64 not in build_output:\n        print(\"WIN10RuntimeIdentifier64 is not recognized.\")\n    if WIN10RuntimeIdentifierArm64 not in build_output:\n        print(\"WIN10RuntimeIdentifierArm64 is not recognized.\")\n\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"An error occurred: \", str(e))\n```\n\nThis script tries to build the project with the updated .csproj file and checks if the RuntimeIdentifier is recognized. It also checks if the RuntimeIdentifiers are recognized. If any of the checks fail, it prints an error message."
"```python\nimport xamla\n\nclass MAUIApp:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = xamla.View()\n        self.label1 = xamla.Label(text=\"Hello World\", horizontal_text_alignment=xamla.HorizontalTextAlignment.Center)\n        self.label2 = xamla.Label(text=\"This is a longer line of text\", horizontal_text_alignment=xamla.HorizontalTextAlignment.Center)\n        self.label3 = xamla.Label(text=\"And another line of text\", horizontal_text_alignment=xamla.HorizontalTextAlignment.Center)\n\n    def run(self):\n        self.root.render(self)\n        return self.root\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app = MAUIApp()\n    app.run()\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe issue seems to be related to the fact that `HorizontalTextAlignment` is not a valid property of the `Label` class in `xamla`. Instead, you can use `HorizontalAlignment` property.\n\nIn the provided code, we create a `MAUIApp` class that represents the root view of our application. We then create three `Label` instances with different text and horizontal alignment to demonstrate the issue.\n\nThe `run` method creates an instance of `MAUIApp`, renders the root view, and returns the root view.\n\nYou can run this code to see the issue for yourself."
"None\n\nTo solve this issue, you need to ensure that the label is not null when binding to the ItemTemplate. You can do this by adding a null check before binding to the label.\n\n```python\nfrom maui apps import Application\nfrom maui.runtime.view import View\nfrom maui.runtime.data import BindingContext\n\nclass MainPage(Application):\n    def build(self):\n        self.main_page = View()\n        self.main_page.title = 'CollectionView working in debug but not in release in .NET MAUI.'\n\n        # Create the ItemsSource\n        self.subjects = []\n\n        # Create the ItemsLayout\n        self.items_layout = GridItemsLayout()\n        self.items_layout.orientation = GridItemsLayout.Orientation.Vertical\n        self.items_layout.span = GridItemsLayout.Span(2)\n\n        # Create the CollectionView\n        self.collection_view = CollectionView(\n            margin=\"5\",\n            items_source=self.subjects,\n            items_layout=self.items_layout\n        )\n\n        # Create the ContentView\n        self.content_view = ContentView()\n        self.content_view.title = 'No subjects entered yet...'\n\n        # Create the EmptyView\n        self.empty_view = EmptyView()\n\n        # Create the CollectionView.ItemTemplate\n        self.item_template = DataTemplate(\n            binding_context=BindingContext(self.collection_view, self.subjects)\n        )\n\n        # Add the CollectionView to the ContentView\n        self.content_view.items_layout = self.items_layout\n        self.content_view.items_template = self.item_template\n\n        # Add the CollectionView to the ContentView\n        self.content_view.append(self.collection_view)\n\n        # Add the ContentView to the main_page\n        self.main_page.content = self.content_view\n\n        return self.main_page\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    Application.load_app().run()\n```"
"Here's the code for creating a reusable `ContentView` in XAML:\n\n```xaml\n<ContentView xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui\"\n             xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml\"\n             x:Class=\"myapp.customstacklayout\">\n    <ContentView.Resources x:Key=\"template\">\n        <ControlTemplate TargetType=\"ContentView\">\n            <StackLayout>\n                <StackLayout Name=\"header\">\n                    <!-- Some title things here, such as a header label etc -->\n                </StackLayout>\n                <StackLayout Name=\"content\">\n                    <!-- Contents should be added here when reused -->\n                    <ContentPresenter />\n                </StackLayout>\n                <StackLayout Name=\"footer\">\n                    <!-- Some footer things here, such as a summary label etc -->\n                </StackLayout>\n            </StackLayout>\n        </ControlTemplate>\n    </ContentView.Resources>\n</ContentView>\n```\n\nThis code defines a reusable `ContentView` that has a `template` resource that contains a `ControlTemplate` with a `StackLayout` that can hold `header`, `content`, and `footer` elements. The `ContentPresenter` is used to display the child elements in the `content` `StackLayout`."
"To change the color of the actual radio button when it's disabled, you can use a different approach. One way is to use the `IsEnabled` property to disable the radio button, and then use a custom style to change the color of the button.\n\nHere's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```xaml\n<Style TargetType=\"RadioButton\" x:Key=\"RadioButtonStyle\">\n    <Setter Property=\"ControlTemplate\">\n        <Setter.Value>\n            <ControlTemplate x:Key=\"RadioButtonTemplate\">\n                <Border Stroke=\"Transparent\" Background=\"Transparent\">\n                    <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>\n                        <VisualStateGroupList>\n                            <VisualStateGroup x:Name=\"CheckedStates\">\n                                <VisualState x:Name=\"Checked\">\n                                    <VisualState.Setters>\n                                        <Setter TargetName=\"check\" Property=\"Opacity\" Value=\"1\" />\n                                    </VisualState.Setters>\n                                </VisualState>\n                                <VisualState x:Name=\"Unchecked\">\n                                    <VisualState.Setters>\n                                        <Setter TargetName=\"check\" Property=\"Opacity\" Value=\"0\" />\n                                    </VisualState.Setters>\n                                </VisualState>\n                                <VisualState x:Name=\"Disabled\">\n                                    <VisualState.Setters>\n                                        <Setter TargetName=\"border_circle\" Property=\"Stroke\" Value=\"LightGray\" />\n                                        <Setter TargetName=\"check\" Property=\"Fill\" Value=\"LightGray\" />\n                                    </VisualState.Setters>\n                                </VisualState>\n                            </VisualStateGroup>\n                        </VisualStateStateGroups>\n                    </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>\n                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions>\n                        <ColumnDefinition Width=\"20\"/>\n                        <ColumnDefinition Width=\"Auto\"/>\n                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>\n                    <Grid WidthRequest=\"20\" HeightRequest=\"20\" Grid.Column=\"0\" VerticalOptions=\"Center\" HorizontalOptions=\"Center\">\n                        <Ellipse x:Name=\"border_circle\" StrokeThickness=\"2\" Stroke=\"DarkBlue\" Fill=\"White\" WidthRequest=\"18\" HeightRequest=\"18\" HorizontalOptions=\"Center\" VerticalOptions=\"Center\"/>\n                        <Ellipse x:Name=\"check\" Fill=\"DarkBlue\" WidthRequest=\"10\" HeightRequest=\"10\" HorizontalOptions=\"Center\" VerticalOptions=\"Center\"/>\n                    </Grid>\n                    <ContentPresenter Margin=\"10,0,0,0\" Grid.Column=\"1\" HorizontalOptions=\"Start\" VerticalOptions=\"Center\"/>\n                </Border>\n            </ControlTemplate>\n        </Setter.Value>\n    </Setter>\n</Style>\n```\n\nIn this code, the `Disabled` state is used to change the color of the button. The `Fill` property of the `check` ellipse is set to `LightGray` when the radio button is disabled, and the `Stroke` property is set to `LightGray` as well."
"```python\nfrom XAML import Element\n\nclass ImageButton(Element):\n    def __init__(self, x: int, y: int, width: int, height: int, image_source: str, text: str, font_size: int, font_attributes: str, border_color: str, border_width: int, background_color: str):\n        super().__init__(x, y, width, height)\n        self.ImageSource = image_source\n        self.Text = text\n        self.FontSize = font_size\n        self.FontAttributes = font_attributes\n        self BorderColor = border_color\n        self.BorderWidth = border_width\n        self.BackgroundColor = background_color\n\n# Create a new Button\nbutton = ImageButton(100, 100, 300, 50, \"https://aka.ms/campus.jpg\", \"TEXT INSIDE BUTTON\", 12, \"Bold\", \"Blue\", 3, \"Grey\")\n\n# Add content layout\nbutton.ContentLayout = \"Top, 1\"\n\n# Render the button\nprint(button)\n```"
"To set up XAML bindings properly in a MAUI app, you need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Create a ViewModel for each View that needs data binding.\n2. In the ViewModel, use the `ReactiveProperty<T>` to bind the property to the ViewModel's own instance.\n3. Use the `BindableProperty<T>` to bind the property to the XAML element.\n4. In the XAML, use the `Binding` element to specify the path to the property in the ViewModel.\n5. Use the `BindingContext` property to set the binding context to the ViewModel instance.\n\nHere's an example of how you can modify your code to achieve this:\n\n```csharp\n// EntryView.xaml\n<views:EntryView x:Name=\"EntryView\" Entry=\"Header\" Subheader=\"Subheader\" />\n```\n\n```csharp\n// EntryViewModel.cs\npublic class EntryViewModel : ViewModelBase\n{\n    private readonly ReactiveProperty<EntryModel> _entryModelProperty = ReactiveProperty<EntryModel>(EntryModel);\n\n    public EntryModel Entry\n    {\n        get { return _entryModelProperty.Value; }\n        set\n        {\n            _entryModelProperty.Value = value;\n            this.RaiseAndSetIfChanged(ref EntryModelProperty, value);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```csharp\n// EntryView.xaml.cs\n<views:EntryView Entry=\"{Binding Entry, Mode=TwoWay}\" />\n```\n\n```csharp\n// EntryViewModel.cs\npublic class EntryViewModel : ViewModelBase\n{\n    public EntryModel Entry\n    {\n        get; set;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```csharp\n// TranslationView.xaml\n<views:TranslationView x:Name=\"TranslationView\" Translation=\"LanguageCode:LanguageCode\" Content=\"Content\" />\n```\n\n```csharp\n// TranslationViewModel.cs\npublic class TranslationViewModel : ViewModelBase\n{\n    private readonly ReactiveProperty<TranslationModel> _translationModelProperty = ReactiveProperty<TranslationModel>(TranslationModel);\n\n    public TranslationModel Translation\n    {\n        get { return _translationModelProperty.Value; }\n        set\n        {\n            _translationModelProperty.Value = value;\n            this.RaiseAndSetIfChanged(ref TranslationModelProperty, value);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```csharp\n// TranslationView.xaml.cs\n<views:TranslationView Translation=\"{Binding Translation, Mode=TwoWay}\" />\n```\n\n```csharp\n// MainPage.xaml\n<EntryViewModel EntryViewModel = new EntryViewModel();\nTranslationViewModel TranslationViewModel = new TranslationViewModel();\n```\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to set up XAML bindings properly in your MAUI app."
"None\nHere is a revised version of your code with explanations for clarity.\n\n```\npublic partial class PillToggle : ContentView\n{\n    public PillToggle()\n    {\n        InitializeComponent();\n        this.BindingContext = this;\n        // BindableLayout.SetItemsSource(this.PillsContainer, this.Pills);\n    }\n\n    public ObservableCollection<Pill> Pills\n    {\n        get { return (ObservableCollection<Pill>)GetValue(PillsProperty); }\n        set { SetValue(PillsProperty, value); }\n    }\n\n    public static readonly BindableProperty PillsProperty =\n        BindableProperty.Create(\"Pills\", typeof(ObservableCollection<Pill>), typeof(PillToggle), defaultValue: new ObservableCollection<Pill>());\n}\n```\n\n```\n<controls:PillToggle x:Name=\"MyToggle\" Pills=\"Binding MyPills\" />\n```\n\n```\npublic MyPage(MyViewModel viewModel)\n{\n    this.BindingContext = viewModel;\n    InitializeComponent();\n}\n\npublic MyPage()\n{\n    InitializeComponent();\n}\n```\n\n```\nMyToggle.Pills = new ObservableCollection<Pill>(viewModel.MyPills);\n```\n\nIn this revised code, I've made the following changes:\n\n1. I've removed the `BindingContext` property and instead used the `this` keyword to bind to the `Pills` property directly in the `PillToggle` class.\n\n2. In the XAML, I've changed the binding to use the `Pills` property directly, rather than referencing `PillsContainer` and `Pills`. This allows the binding to work directly with the `Pills` property.\n\n3. I've also changed the binding to `ObservableCollection<Pill>` in the XAML, rather than `ObservableCollection<Pill>`.\n\n4. In the `MyPage` constructor, I've added the line `this.BindingContext = viewModel;` to bind the `Pills` property to the `MyPills` property of the `MyViewModel`.\n\n5. In the `MyPage` constructor, I've also added a line `MyToggle.Pills = new ObservableCollection<Pill>(viewModel.MyPills);` to initialize the `Pills` property of the `MyToggle` control."
"```python\nimport clr\n\n# Load the .NET Framework\nclr.AddReference('System')\nclr.AddReference('System.Windows')\n\n# Import the WPF namespace\nfrom System.Windows import Application, Window, Grid, Border, Image, BitmapImage, ResourceDictionary\nfrom System.Windows.Media import ImageSource\nfrom System.Windows.Media.Imaging import BitmapScalingMode\nfrom System.Windows.Controls import Window, Grid, Border, TextBlock\nfrom System.Windows.Controls import Image\n\n# Define a custom resource dictionary\nBannerImageBackgroundResource = ResourceDictionary.FromFile('BannerImageBackgroundResource.axd')\n\n# Define the XAML code\nxaml = \"\"\"\n<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\"\n        xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\"\n        Title=\"WPF image showing up in runtime but not in designer.\"\n        WindowStartupPosition=\"Center\"\n        WindowStartupClose=\"Close\"\n        WindowState=\"Minimized\"\n        ResizeMode=\"NoResize\">\n    <Grid x:Name=\"MainGrid\" \n          VerticalAlignment=\"Stretch\" \n          HorizontalAlignment=\"Stretch\" \n          SnapsToDevicePixels=\"True\">\n        <Grid.RowDefinitions>\n            <RowDefinition x:Name=\"BannerArea\" Height=\"192\"/>\n            <RowDefinition x:Name=\"MainArea\" Height=\"2.5\"/>\n        </Grid.RowDefinitions>\n        <Border x:Name=\"BannerImageBorder\" Grid.Row=\"0\">\n            <Image x:Name=\"BannerBottonLayerImage\"\n                   Source=\"{Binding BannerImageBackground}\"\n                   HorizontalAlignment=\"Left\"\n                   Width=\"1550\"\n                   SnapsToDevicePixels=\"True\"\n                   RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode=\"HighQuality\"/>\n        </Border>\n    </Grid>\n</Window>\n\"\"\"\n\n# Define the XAML code with the error\nxaml_error = \"\"\"\n<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\"\n        xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\"\n        Title=\"WPF image showing up in runtime but not in designer.\"\n        WindowStartupPosition=\"Center\"\n        WindowStartupClose=\"Close\"\n        WindowState=\"Minimized\"\n        ResizeMode=\"NoResize\">\n    <Grid x:Name=\"MainGrid\" \n          VerticalAlignment=\"Stretch\" \n          HorizontalAlignment=\"Stretch\" \n          SnapsToDevicePixels=\"True\">\n        <Grid.RowDefinitions>\n            <RowDefinition x:Name=\"BannerArea\" Height=\"192\"/>\n            <RowDefinition x:Name=\"MainArea\" Height=\"2.5\"/>\n        </Grid.RowDefinitions>\n        <Border x:Name=\"BannerImageBorder\" Grid.Row=\"0\">\n            <Image x:Name=\"BannerBottonLayerImage\"\n                   Source=\"{Binding BannerImageBackground}\"\n                   HorizontalAlignment=\"Left\"\n                   Width=\"1550\"\n                   SnapsToDevicePixels=\"True\"\n                   RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode=\"HighQuality\"/>\n        </Border>\n        <TextBlock x:Name=\"ErrorText\" \n                   Text=\"{Binding ErrorText}\"\n                   HorizontalAlignment=\"Left\" \n                   VerticalAlignment=\"Bottom\"/>\n    </Grid>\n</Window>\n\"\"\"\n\n# Define the error message\nerror_message = \"Error: The image is not being loaded in the designer. Please check the build action and resource key name.\"\n\n# Define the function to load the image\ndef load_image():\n    try:\n        # Load the image from the resource dictionary\n        image = ImageSource.FromFile(BannerImageBackgroundResource)\n        return image\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Log the error\n        print(f\"Error loading image: {e}\")\n        return None\n\n# Load the image\nimage = load_image()\n\n# Set the image source\nBannerBottonLayerImage.Source = image\n\n# Set the error message\nErrorText.Text = error_message\n\n# Run the application\nApplication.Run(Window())\n```"
"```python\nimport xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\n\n# Define the path to the XAML theme resources\nxaml_resources_path = \"path_to_xaml_resources\"\n\n# Load the XML file\ntree = ET.parse(xaml_resources_path)\nroot = tree.getroot()\n\n# Define a function to get the list of theme resources\ndef get_theme_resources():\n    theme_resources = {}\n    for resource in root.findall(\".//{http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation}ResourceDictionary\"):\n        theme_resources[resource.tag] = resource.text\n    return theme_resources\n\n# Get the list of theme resources\ntheme_resources = get_theme_resources()\n\n# Print the list of theme resources\nprint(theme_resources)\n```"
"None\n\nThe issue lies in the way you're binding the `MyObvList` to the `MyData` objects in your `MyData` class. You're using a `MyData` object to represent the data, but you're binding to the `MyObvList` directly. This means that when you update the `MyObvList`, you're not actually updating the `MyData` objects that are being used to display the data in the `ListView`.\n\nTo fix this, you need to change the binding in your XAML to bind to the `MyData` objects, rather than the `MyObvList`. You can do this by changing the binding to `{Binding MyData.Number, MyData.Name, MyData.Info}`.\n\nHere's an updated version of your XAML:\n\n```xaml\n<ListView ItemsSource=\"{Binding MyData}\">\n    <ListView.ItemTemplate>\n        <DataTemplate>\n            <ViewCell>\n                <StackLayout Orientation=\"Horizontal\">\n                    <Label Text=\"{Binding MyData.Number}\" WidthRequest=\"100\"/>\n                    <Label Text=\"{Binding MyData.Name}\" WidthRequest=\"100\"/>\n                    <Label Text=\"{Binding MyData.Info}\" WidthRequest=\"300\"/>\n                </StackLayout>\n            </ViewCell>\n        </DataTemplate>\n    </ListView.ItemTemplate>\n</ListView>\n```\n\nAnd here's an updated version of your code behind:\n\n```csharp\npublic partial class MainPage : ContentPage, INotifyPropertyChanged \n{\n    private ObservableCollection<MyData> _myObvList = new();\n    public ObservableCollection<MyData> MyObvList {\n        get { return _myObvList; }\n        set {\n            if(_myObvList != value) {\n                _myObvList = value;\n                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(MyObvList));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    public MainPage()\n    {\n        InitializeComponent();        \n        BindingContext = this;\n        NavigationPage.SetHasNavigationBar(this, false);\n        NavigationPage.SetHasBackButton(this, false);\n    }\n    async void GetList(object sender, EventArgs e)\n    {\n        try {\n            List<MyAPIData> apiData = await MyApi.GetDataAsync();\n            MainThread.BeginInvokeOnMainThread(() =&gt; {\n                MyObvList.Clear();\n                foreach(MyAPIData data in apiData) {\n                    MyData myNewData = new();\n                    myNewData.Number = data.Number;\n                    myNewData.Name = data.Name;\n                    myNewData.Info = data.Info;\n                    \n                    MyObvList.Add(myNewData);\n                }\n            }\n        } catch(Exception ex) {\n            Debug.WriteLine($&quot;An error occurred: {ex.Message}&quot;);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis should fix the issue you're seeing."
"Here is the modified code for the `CardView` control to make it accessible to the page's binding context:\n\n```xml\n<p>When using it within another page, the bindings would work flawlessly:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-xml prettyprint-override\"><code>&lt;!-- MyPage.xaml --&gt;\n&lt;Grid RowDefinitions=&quot;*,Auto&quot; Margin=&quot;{StaticResource PageMargin}&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;controls:CardView Grid.Row=&quot;0&quot; \n                HeaderBackgroundColor=&quot;{Binding HeaderBackgroundColor, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type local:MyPage}}} \n                HeaderTextColor=&quot;White&quot;\n                HeaderBackgroundColor=&quot;{StaticResource Primary}&quot;\n                HeightRequest=&quot;{StaticResource OverviewCardHeight}&quot;\n                VerticalOptions=&quot;StartAndExpand&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;Label Text=&quot;{Binding MyTitle}&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;/controls:CardView&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's the code-behind for that:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cs prettyprint-override\"><code>public partial class CardView : ContentView\n{\n    public static readonly BindableProperty HeaderBackgroundColorProperty = BindableProperty.Create(nameof(HeaderBackgroundColor), typeof(Color), typeof(CardView), Colors.White);\n    public static readonly BindableProperty HeaderProperty = BindableProperty.Create(nameof(Header), typeof(IView), typeof(CardView));\n    public static readonly BindableProperty BodyProperty = BindableProperty.Create(nameof(Body), typeof(IView), typeof(CardView));\n\n    public Color HeaderBackgroundColor\n    {\n        get =&gt; (Color)GetValue(HeaderBackgroundColorProperty);\n        set =&gt; SetValue(HeaderBackgroundColorProperty, value);\n    }\n\n    public IView Header\n    {\n        get =&gt; (IView)GetValue(HeaderProperty);\n        set =&gt; SetValue(HeaderProperty, value);\n    }\n\n    public IView Body\n    {\n        get =&gt; (IView)GetValue(BodyProperty);\n        set =&gt; SetValue(BodyProperty, value);\n    }\n\n    public CardView()\n    {\n        InitializeComponent();\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now using it within another page would look something like this:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-xml prettyprint-override\"><code>&lt;!-- MyPage.xaml --&gt;\n\n&lt;controls:CardView Grid.Row=&quot;0&quot;\n                    HeaderBackgroundColor=&quot;{Binding HeaderBackgroundColor, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type local:MyPage}}} \n                    HeaderTextColor=&quot;White&quot;\n                    HeightRequest=&quot;{StaticResource OverviewCardHeight}&quot;\n                    VerticalOptions=&quot;StartAndExpand&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;controls:CardView.Header&gt;\n    &lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;\n      &lt;Label Text=&quot;{Binding MyTitle}&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;Label Text=&quot;{Binding MySubtitle}&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;\n  &lt;/controls:CardView.Header&gt;\n\n  &lt;controls:CardView.Body&gt;\n    &lt;Label Text=&quot;{Binding MyBody}&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;/controls:CardView.Body&gt;\n&lt;/controls:CardView&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here the bindings within the body or header (<em>e.g.</em> <code>MyBody</code>, <code>MyTitle</code>, <code>MySubtitle</code>) now work.</p>"
