"```python\nclass Composition:\n    def __init__(self, subject, attributes):\n        self.subject = subject\n        self.attributes = attributes\n\nclass Aggregation:\n    def __init__(self, subject, attributes, relationship):\n        self.subject = subject\n        self.attributes = attributes\n        self.relationship = relationship\n\nclass Subject:\n    pass\n\ndef cast_composition_to_aggregation(subject, composition):\n    return Aggregation(subject, composition.attributes, composition.relationship)\n\n# Usage\nsubject = Subject()\ncomposition = Composition(Subject, [\"color\", \"size\"])\naggregation = cast_composition_to_aggregation(subject, composition)\nprint(aggregation)\n```\n\nComposition is labelled as a subset of aggregation in some illustrations because it shares the same basic characteristics as aggregation, such as having a subject and attributes, but it lacks the explicit dependency between the subject and its attributes, hence it is considered a subset of aggregation."
