"import requests\n\ndef consume_ws_with_security():\n    # Set up the client credentials\n    username = \"YourUsername\"\n    password = \"YourPassword\"\n    client_credentials = {\n        \"username\": username,\n        \"password\": password\n    }\n\n    # Set the WS URL\n    ws_url = \"https://*************/DocumentUploadWS.wsdl\"\n\n    # Set the WS security details\n    security_details = {\n        \"usernameToken\": {\n            \"id\": \"UsernameToken-49\",\n            \"username\": username,\n            \"password\": password\n        }\n    }\n\n    # Set the WS binding details\n    binding_details = {\n        \"name\": \"DocumentUploadWSSoap11\",\n        \"security\": {\n            \"mode\": \"TransportWithMessageCredential\"\n        }\n    }\n\n    # Set the WS endpoint details\n    endpoint_details = {\n        \"binding\": {\n            \"name\": \"DocumentUploadWSSoap11\",\n            \"security\": binding_details[\"security\"]\n        },\n        \"headers\": {\n            \"wsse:Security\": {\n                \"xmlns\": \"http://docs.oasis-open.org/wss/2004/01/oasis-200401-wss-wssecurity-secext-1.0.xsd\",\n                \"UsernameToken\": {\n                    \"id\": \"UsernameToken-49\",\n                    \"username\": username,\n                    \"password\": password\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    # Set the WS client details\n    client_details = {\n        \"endpoint\": endpoint_details,\n        \"binding\": binding_details,\n        \"headers\": security_details\n    }\n\n    # Set the WS client credentials\n    client = requests.Session()\n    client.username_password = client_details[\"client_credentials\"]\n\n    # Set the WS request details\n    request_details = {\n        \"method\": \"GET\",\n        \"uri\": ws_url,\n        \"headers\": client_details[\"headers\"]\n    }\n\n    # Send the WS request\n    response = client.request(**request_details)\n\n    # Check the response status\n    if response.status_code == 200:\n        print(\"The WS request was successful.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"The WS request failed with status code:\", response.status_code)\n\nconsume_ws_with_security()"
"Here is the corrected Python code for the 'heartbeat' service:\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\n# Create a logger\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n# Define a function to handle heartbeat requests\ndef heartbeat():\n    \"\"\"Return a 200 status code\"\"\"\n    logger.info(\"checking heartbeat\")\n    return 200\n\n# Define a function to handle requests to the heartbeat endpoint\n@app.route(\"/heartbeat\")\ndef handle_heartbeat():\n    \"\"\"Return a 200 status code\"\"\"\n    return heartbeat()\n\n# Define a function to handle requests to the status endpoint\n@app.route(\"/status\")\ndef handle_status():\n    \"\"\"Return a 200 status code\"\"\"\n    return 200\n\n# Define a function to handle requests to the hello endpoint\n@app.route(\"/hello\")\ndef handle_hello():\n    \"\"\"Return 'Hello World'\"\"\"\n    return \"Hello World\"\n```\n\nIn this corrected code:\n\n*   We define a `heartbeat` function that returns a 200 status code.\n*   We define a `handle_heartbeat` function that calls the `heartbeat` function and returns the result.\n*   We define a `handle_status` function that also calls the `heartbeat` function and returns a 200 status code.\n*   We define a `handle_hello` function that returns the string \"Hello World\".\n\nWe then use the Flask framework's built-in `@app.route()` decorator to define routes for each endpoint. The `@CrossOrigin` decorator is not necessary in Python, as Flask automatically enables CORS by default.\n\nNote that this code assumes you are using Flask 2.x. If you are using an older version, you may need to modify the code accordingly."
"None\n\nTo avoid waiting on sending HTTP requests via Oracle database, you can consider the following approaches:\n\n- Approach 1: Application inserts a row to a table, trigger after insert calls a DB function that uses UTL_HTTP to perform the request.\n\n  Advantages:\n  - Can be more efficient as it avoids the overhead of a database request.\n  - Does not require the application and database to be aware of the response.\n\n  Pitfalls:\n  - May not be suitable for large-scale applications as it can lead to performance issues.\n  - Requires careful planning and testing to ensure that the UTL_HTTP library is used correctly.\n\n- Approach 2: Application calls a DB function that uses UTL_HTTP to perform the request via a select statement.\n\n  Advantages:\n  - Can be more efficient as it avoids the overhead of a database request.\n  - Suitable for large-scale applications where performance is critical.\n\n  Pitfalls:\n  - Requires the application and database to be aware of the response, which may not be desirable in some cases.\n  - May require additional logic to handle the response and update the application state."
