"None\n \nTo retrieve an email profile picture from an email address, you typically need to use a service that has a predefined set of images associated with each email address. Here's an example of how you can achieve this using Python:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nfrom email.parser import Parser\n\ndef get_email_profile_picture(email):\n    # Parse the email\n    parser = Parser()\n    email_string = parser.parsestring(email)\n    body = email_string.get_payload()\n\n    # Send a request to Gravatar to get the image\n    gravatar_url = f\"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/{email}\"\n    headers = {\"Accept\": \"image/png\", \"Accept-Encoding\": \"gzip, deflate\"}\n    response = requests.get(gravatar_url, headers=headers)\n\n    # Check if the response was successful\n    if response.status_code == 200:\n        # Return the image data\n        return response.content\n    else:\n        # Raise an error if the response was not successful\n        raise Exception(f\"Failed to retrieve Gravatar image for email {email}\")\n\n# Example usage:\nemail = \"your_email@example.com\"\nprofile_picture = get_email_profile_picture(email)\nif profile_picture:\n    with open(\"email_profile_picture.png\", \"wb\") as file:\n        file.write(profile_picture)\n    print(f\"Profile picture saved as email_profile_picture.png\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"Failed to retrieve Gravatar image for email {email}\")\n```\n\nThis code sends a GET request to the Gravatar URL for the provided email address, which returns a PNG image. The image data is then saved to a file. If the request fails, an error message is printed."
